# Reproducible build output.
#
# Scoped to the application project rather than written as dev/**/bin/. That
# pattern also matched dev/core/src/bin/, which is not build output at all - it
# is where every Rust binary lives, including the agent that owns the radio. The
# whole of it was therefore missing from the repository, so a fresh clone could
# not build the program and nothing said why.
dev/app/**/bin/
dev/app/**/obj/
dev/tools/**/bin/
dev/tools/**/obj/
dev/core/target/
dev/build/
dev/artifacts/
dev/captures/*backup.json

# Machine-local rollback state and optional downloaded installers
release/runtime-data/*.json
release/dependencies/*.exe
release/dependencies/*.zip
release/dependencies/VB-CABLE/

# Upload this as a GitHub Release asset instead of committing it. The whole
# ready-to-run package is already in release/, so a copy of it in the repository
# root is both duplicated and, once a version has moved on, actively misleading:
# somebody downloading the stale zip gets old binaries from a repository whose
# source has changed underneath them.
OpenLEAudio-*-win-x64.zip
OpenLEAudio-*.zip
dev/.claude/
dev/captures/

# Local diagnostics, caches and old binary backups.
/release/.*-staging/
/release/runtime-data/*
!/release/runtime-data/README.txt
/release/OpenLEAudio/logs/
/dev/backup/
/dev/backups/
/dev/tests/**/bin/
/dev/tests/**/obj/
/dev/tests/*.log
/konzole*.txt
/soubory bluetooth trace na vývoj/
OpenLEAudio-*.zip.sha256
